From f7cdaf18ee4e1397d3dfe01caad14f7233cfcdf0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "mlaify-agent (Claude)" <mlaify-agent@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 18:09:28 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] Suppressions: one line convention, and `expires` is part
 of the trusted key

Two more ways a pull request could silence its own finding under
--suppress-from-ref (GHSA-677m-4884-vg22), found while fixing the
adjacent-line case:

- Findings count lines with "\n" only (srcpaths.line_number), but inline
  directives and gitref.added_lines used str.splitlines(), which also
  breaks on \x0c, \x0b, \x1c-\x1e, \x85, U+2028 and U+2029. One such
  character in a comment above a base-trusted directive shifted the
  directive by one line in the suppression view only, onto the line the
  PR added below the covered one; the new finding was suppressed and the
  gate stayed green. New srcpaths.split_lines() is the single convention;
  suppress.py, gitref.py and fingerprint.py use it, and so do the
  detectors and helpers that number or index lines (supply_chain, sbom,
  workflow_scanner, webhook_verification, route_auth_fusion, taint), so a
  directive always covers the line it sits above.
- _selector_key ignored `expires`, so a PR could set a base entry's
  expiry to 2099, or delete it, and the expired entry was applied again
  with no warning. `expires` is now part of the key: an edited expiry is
  "changed since the ref" and pending.

action.yml gains an opt-in `fail-on-new-suppression` input that passes
--fail-on-new-suppression, which catches a baseline-active finding
becoming suppressed by any route.

Tests: a parametrized gate test with a form feed, U+2028, \x0b and \x85
comment above a trusted directive, and two expiry tests (extended,
removed).

